format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radioactivity

objective u2-random-single
statement: Know that you cannot predict when one particular nucleus will decay, although a large group follows a pattern.
misconception: If you watch a nucleus closely you can tell when it will change.

item u2-l1-choice-next
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-wikipedia-radioactive-decay

prompt:
    Look at a group of unstable nuclei. Could you tell in advance which one will change next?

options:
    {"id":"no","label":"No: it is random for each nucleus"}
    {"id":"first","label":"Yes: the one at the front always goes first","note":"Position does not matter. Each unstable nucleus has the same chance."}
    {"id":"oldest","label":"Yes: the oldest one goes first","note":"A nucleus does not get older or due. It has the same chance every time."}

answer: no

explanation:
    No. When one particular nucleus changes cannot be predicted. Each unstable nucleus has the same chance, so you cannot call the next one.

item u2-l1-tom-chance
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-wikipedia-radioactive-decay

statement:
    You can say how likely a nucleus is to change, but not exactly when it will.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. An unstable nucleus has a chance of changing, but when one particular nucleus changes cannot be predicted.

item u2-x-tom-exact
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-wikipedia-radioactive-decay

statement:
    If you watch one unstable nucleus closely, you can tell the exact second it will change.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. When one particular nucleus changes cannot be predicted.

item u2-x-two-nuclei
type: choice
difficulty: 2
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-wikipedia-radioactive-decay

prompt:
    In a sample, one nucleus changed today. What can you say about another unstable nucleus in the same sample?

options:
    {"id":"unknown","label":"It could change today, tomorrow or much later: you cannot tell"}
    {"id":"tomorrow","label":"It will change tomorrow","note":"Nothing fixes the moment for one nucleus."}
    {"id":"together","label":"It changes at the same moment as the first","note":"Each nucleus changes at its own random moment."}

answer: unknown

explanation:
    You cannot say when another particular nucleus will change. Only a large group follows a pattern.
